    "design_critique": "1) The central premise is contradicted by the race data. The draft says the outer sensors lose the tape at about 0.03 m, so line loss causes the exits. But line_loss_events is 0.0 in every trial of every raced design (iterations 2 and 5, 120 trials), and max_error sits at the 0.06 m limit while the line is still being read. The robot leaves the track with the line visible, so loss of sensing is not the failure mode. The draft's main fix targets a cause the data does not support.

2) The geometry claim is wrong. With a 12 mm footprint and a 25 mm tape, a sensor at lateral x reads tape when the robot offset d satisfies |x - d| < 0.0185. In iteration 2, the outer sensor at x = 0.04 read tape up to d of about 0.0585, just inside the 0.06 m limit. The draft's outer sensor at x = 0.06 reads tape only for d between about 0.0415 and 0.0785, so it is blank over the normal operating band (d below about 0.04) and reaches past the limit. Widening the span does not extend the useful visible range. It moves the outer edge beyond the point where the robot already exits.

3) The gain argument is internally inconsistent. The signal is a reading-weighted average of fixed index weights (-2 to 2), so the sensor x positions change only how fast the signal moves with offset. Spacing rises from 0.02 to 0.03 m, so signal per metre falls to roughly two-thirds of iteration 2's value. The rationale says the gain falls, then keeps kp at 3.0 and says this holds the gain 'near iteration 2's level'. At 3.0 the effective gain is about 0.67 of iteration 2, not the same. Holding it would need kp near 4.5. The same error applies to kd,
